     <rdf:li>Amer AbuGhazaleh and Khaled R. Ahmed</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">BactSegNet: A Growth-Stage-Aware</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">BactSegNet: A Growth-Stage-Aware</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Searched Instance-Segmentation Model for Salmonella and Escherichia coli Microcolonies in Pure-Culture Bright-Field Microscopy with a Food-Matrix Surrogate</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Searched Instance-Segmentation Model for Salmonella and Escherichia coli Microcolonies in Pure-Culture Bright-Field Microscopy with a Food-Matrix Surrogate</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>deep learning; instance segmentation; foodborne pathogen detection; bacterial microscopy; neural architecture search; food safety; microcolony segmentation; Salmonella; Escherichia coli; computer vision</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Instance segmentation of small</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Instance segmentation of small</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">dense</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">dense</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">low-contrast microcolonies against confounding food matrices limits early microscopy-based screening for Salmonella and E. coli. We present BactSegNet</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">low-contrast microcolonies against confounding food matrices limits early microscopy-based screening for Salmonella and E. coli. We present BactSegNet</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">combining EfficientNetV2</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">combining EfficientNetV2</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">BiFPN</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">BiFPN</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">selective-kernel attention</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">selective-kernel attention</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and growth-stage FiLM conditioning based on a learnable embedding of recorded acquisition stage. BE-NAS explored a ${2304}$-configuration search space and evaluated $71$ unique architectures. Across twelve retrained candidates</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and growth-stage FiLM conditioning based on a learnable embedding of recorded acquisition stage. BE-NAS explored a ${2304}$-configuration search space and evaluated $71$ unique architectures. Across twelve retrained candidates</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">proxy and full-training rankings agreed (Spearman $\rho =0.867$ for mAP@50 and $\rho =0.790$ for mAP@50--95). Across the $71$ architectures evaluated in the BE-NAS proxy search</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">proxy and full-training rankings agreed (Spearman $\rho =0.867$ for mAP@50 and $\rho =0.790$ for mAP@50--95). Across the $71$ architectures evaluated in the BE-NAS proxy search</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">three-seed proxy fitness showed high reliability ($\mathrm {ICC}(1</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">three-seed proxy fitness showed high reliability ($\mathrm {ICC}(1</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">1)=0.91$; $213$ seed-level evaluations)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">1)=0.91$; $213$ seed-level evaluations)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">while phenotype caching reduced $180$ scheduled evaluations to $71$ unique trainings ($213$ rather than $540$ seed-level proxy runs). On $14{,}318$ expert-verified polygon instances spanning six stages</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">while phenotype caching reduced $180$ scheduled evaluations to $71$ unique trainings ($213$ rather than $540$ seed-level proxy runs). On $14{,}318$ expert-verified polygon instances spanning six stages</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">two species</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">two species</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and onion-present/absent conditions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and onion-present/absent conditions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">BactSegNet-L achieved mask mAP@50 = $0.9636$ and mAP@50--95 = $0.7154$</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">BactSegNet-L achieved mask mAP@50 = $0.9636$ and mAP@50--95 = $0.7154$</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">$3.56$ percentage points above a multi-seed YOLOv8 baseline and higher mAP@50 than sixteen class-aware single-run references across three paradigms. A capacity-preserving stage-perturbation control reduced mAP@50 from $0.9636\pm 0.0049$ with correct stage assignments to $0.9487\pm 0.0040$ after shuffling image--stage correspondence across three seeds. Gains reached $8.66$ pp for Salmonella at $1.5$ h and $4.90$ pp on onion. Across $2756$ matched instances</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">$3.56$ percentage points above a multi-seed YOLOv8 baseline and higher mAP@50 than sixteen class-aware single-run references across three paradigms. A capacity-preserving stage-perturbation control reduced mAP@50 from $0.9636\pm 0.0049$ with correct stage assignments to $0.9487\pm 0.0040$ after shuffling image--stage correspondence across three seeds. Gains reached $8.66$ pp for Salmonella at $1.5$ h and $4.90$ pp on onion. Across $2756$ matched instances</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">area MAPE was $12.15\%$ with $r=0.997$</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">area MAPE was $12.15\%$ with $r=0.997$</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">dominated by the largest instances. BactSegNet-Efficient retained $0.9510$ mAP@50 with $19.1\%$ fewer FLOPs at $43.9$ fps. The main dataset comprises pure-culture frames; an independent, expert-annotated mixed-culture evaluation (400 images, 3.5 and 4.0 h) yielded mask mAP@50=$0.8857\pm 0.0206$ across three seeds without retraining. Recorded growth-stage metadata are required at inference, unlike the image-only comparison models. These results support early, physically calibrated microcolony segmentation while distinguishing the original pure-culture evaluation from the additional mixed-culture test.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">dominated by the largest instances. BactSegNet-Efficient retained $0.9510$ mAP@50 with $19.1\%$ fewer FLOPs at $43.9$ fps. The main dataset comprises pure-culture frames; an independent, expert-annotated mixed-culture evaluation (400 images, 3.5 and 4.0 h) yielded mask mAP@50=$0.8857\pm 0.0206$ across three seeds without retraining. Recorded growth-stage metadata are required at inference, unlike the image-only comparison models. These results support early, physically calibrated microcolony segmentation while distinguishing the original pure-culture evaluation from the additional mixed-culture test.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
